    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Machu Picchu was a royal estate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperors, used for about 80 years and home to some 750 people, most of them retainers who came from many parts of the empire.</li><li>Local farmers knew and worked its terraces, and Agustín Lizárraga reached the citadel in 1902; Hiram Bingham brought it to international attention in 1911.</li><li>Bingham's excavations took thousands of objects to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a>, which agreed only in 2010 to return them; the last reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 2012.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1902 Agustín Lizárraga wrote his name and the year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> on the central window of the Temple of the Three Windows, where Hiram Bingham found and photographed it in 1911.</p></aside>
    </section>

